      "id": "source-037",
      "number": 37,
      "title": "Autoencoder",
      "asset": "assets/slides/slide-037.svg",
      "sha256": "a1fca0d34c23a32e2acb58ed18eb782e84cd09de6c954d517ea26641952ec792",
      "source_xml": "ppt/slides/slide37.xml",
      "source_xml_sha256": "15e4f5d5c57a4e810706af561c95cf85b7d722e3505c6760b9dda873424cb2c0",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Autoencoder",
        "37",
        "Autoencoder is a neural network designed to learn an identity function in an unsupervised way to reconstruct the original input.",
        "Reducing the Dimensionality of Data with Neural Networks",
        "The encoder network essentially accomplishes the dimensionality reduction, just like we use Principal Component Analysis (PCA)."
      ],
      "links": [
        {
          "uri": "https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~hinton/absps/science.pdf",
          "rect": [
            897.28003,
            388.47998,
            312.31999,
            50.56002
          ]
        },
        {
          "uri": "https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~hinton/absps/science.pdf",
          "rect": [
            897.28003,
            417.60002,
            312.31999,
            21.43998
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "source-038",
      "number": 38,
      "title": "Autoencoder",
      "asset": "assets/slides/slide-038.svg",
      "sha256": "281efd3be8453cd9dfe8847c75fc53387a7d15a8ef60b17a0b371973fafe3d79",
      "source_xml": "ppt/slides/slide38.xml",
      "source_xml_sha256": "4d6500ce4c764f870b594ba99677a6f2d00a130b378979e729d92e40975e29b7",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Autoencoder",
        "The model contains an encoder function 𝑔(⋅) parameterized by 𝜙 and a decoder function 𝑓(⋅) parameterized by 𝜽",
        "The low-dimensional code learned for input 𝒙 in the bottleneck layer is 𝒛=𝑔𝜙𝒙 and the reconstructed input is 𝒙′=𝑓𝜃(𝑔𝜙(𝒙))",
        "The parameters (𝝓,𝜽) are learned together to output a reconstructed data sample same as the original input",
        "38"
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "id": "source-039",
      "number": 39,
      "title": "Autoencoder denoising version",
      "asset": "assets/slides/slide-039.svg",
      "sha256": "703bd5e0316fb573d2381c685e7a96581320f626c22d43de5673eeacd7736989",
      "source_xml": "ppt/slides/slide39.xml",
      "source_xml_sha256": "50dec01a05399077ccc868684c5554e11866b31be52c209d26eec4db8690c883",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Autoencoder denoising version",
        "The standard autoencoder quantifies the difference between two vectors, such as cross entropy when the activation function is sigmoid, or as simple as MSE loss:",
        "𝐿𝐴𝐸𝜃,𝜙=1𝑛𝑖=1𝑛𝒙(𝑖)−𝑓𝜃(𝑔𝜙(𝒙(𝑖)))2",
        "The denoising version tries to avoid overfitting and improve the robustness",
        "𝐿𝐷𝐴𝐸𝜃,𝜙=1𝑛𝑖=1𝑛𝒙(𝑖)−𝑓𝜃(𝑔𝜙(𝒙(𝑖)))2",
        "39"
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "id": "source-040",
      "number": 40,
      "title": "Variational Autoencoder (VAE)",
      "asset": "assets/slides/slide-040.svg",
      "sha256": "412953104b485cf6c7304c25fc525e7189fec324beeae48e93dcbb720dfb2ebf",
      "source_xml": "ppt/slides/slide40.xml",
      "source_xml_sha256": "fc5d2e9ac5efb085a72955e45dd2dfd917bf39bbcb53893d9bcdbcc5df1e1e0b",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Variational Autoencoder (VAE)",
        "Autoencoders",
        "learn a low dimensional representation of the input, but hard to work with as a generative model",
        "one of the key limitations of autoencoders is that we have no way of sampling from them!",
        "Variational autoencoders (VAEs)",
        "by contrast learn a continuous latent space that is easy to sample from",
        "can generate new data (e.g. images) by sampling from the learned generative model",
        "40"
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "id": "source-041",
      "number": 41,
      "title": "Variational Autoencoder (VAE)",
      "asset": "assets/slides/slide-041.svg",
      "sha256": "8a710e8890d1796d9bf68c96a92a63acacd3146574b143f0b61dbe6758f28964",
      "source_xml": "ppt/slides/slide41.xml",
      "source_xml_sha256": "179c5fdf01712cb9f2d486310188e656c4cdca6d994286739df7b5b606008295",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Variational Autoencoder (VAE)",
        "Neural Network Perspective",
        "We can view a variational autoencoder (VAE) as an autoencoder consisting of two neural networks",
        "VAEs (as autoencoders) define two distributions",
        "encoder: 𝑞𝝓𝒛𝒙",
        "decoder: 𝑝𝜽𝒙𝒛",
        "We can also view the VAE from the perspective of variational inference",
        "Model: 𝑞𝝓𝒛𝒙",
        "Variational approximation: 𝑝𝜽𝒙𝒛",
        "41"
      ],
      "links": []
    },
