      "title": "VQ-VAE",
      "asset": "assets/slides/slide-047.svg",
      "sha256": "e1abb730f18f5cb902e94cc60b9b2ca5e7ad3f935bde9ce6a811b73d53bfb585",
      "source_xml": "ppt/slides/slide47.xml",
      "source_xml_sha256": "5cfe5a5c2e3348cb91dec7d324c14599755050d45efa32313ca4940c49b12402",
      "paragraphs": [
        "VQ-VAE",
        "Vector Quantized VAE: model learns a discrete latent variable by the encoder, since discrete representations may be a more natural fit for problems like language, speech, reasoning, etc.",
        "47"
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "id": "source-048",
      "number": 48,
      "title": "VQ-VAE",
      "asset": "assets/slides/slide-048.svg",
      "sha256": "61b13dae0c200c6a56a17bb9c8aebebf8356de143ce0aff218d8467511fa509c",
      "source_xml": "ppt/slides/slide48.xml",
      "source_xml_sha256": "beb9701908894906ac025161715b951e73d805c0f6b7f10a096ebbf774b18a23",
      "paragraphs": [
        "VQ-VAE",
        " latent embedding space (also known as “codebook”): 𝒆∈𝑅𝐾×𝐷",
        "𝐾 is the number of latent variable categories",
        "𝐷 is the embedding size. An individual embedding vector is 𝒆𝑖∈𝑅𝐷",
        "Encoder 𝐸(𝒙) output  goes through a nearest-neighbor lookup to match to one of 𝐾 embedding vectors ",
        "matched code vector becomes the input for the decoder 𝐷(⋅)",
        "48"
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "id": "source-049",
      "number": 49,
      "title": "DDPMs",
      "asset": "assets/slides/slide-049.svg",
      "sha256": "15018cd4b47ade11e175177166502ff72cda42e6ea89a36f3010447825d2c798",
      "source_xml": "ppt/slides/slide49.xml",
      "source_xml_sha256": "6ea6b1d1a61bde6d5e2d469398c652ce2394110ffc2a3aa58d7d159a8b35605c",
      "paragraphs": [
        "DDPMs",
        "Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPMs): a class of likelihood-based models that generate samples by gradually removing noise from a signal.",
        "Given a dataset of samples 𝒙 drawn from 𝑝data(𝒙), learn a model 𝑝𝜃𝒙≈ 𝑝data(𝒙) such that we can:",
        "generate new samples 𝒙∼𝑝𝜃(𝒙)",
        "estimate the probability density of the sample 𝑝𝜃(𝒙)",
        "49"
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "id": "source-050",
      "number": 50,
      "title": "DDPMs",
      "asset": "assets/slides/slide-050.svg",
      "sha256": "7b12bfb4399f770e9c4c35b20abc89e3dda09122931727db4ec896c9dab65f00",
      "source_xml": "ppt/slides/slide50.xml",
      "source_xml_sha256": "bb89364d14af1cf3c8dda09954108dfee69cb72f095c2828ec85000d2143ca20",
      "paragraphs": [
        "DDPMs",
        "Forward diffusion process: Corrupt the image till it becomes Gaussian noise (Fixed process)",
        "Reverse diffusion process: Gradually remove the noise till you recover the image (Learned process)",
        "50"
      ],
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "id": "source-051",
      "number": 51,
      "title": "DDPMs",
      "asset": "assets/slides/slide-051.svg",
      "sha256": "5642e5ee050a9ed4568cbd247e5f3e5a91ff99e0a5e331306e07912529e8cfec",
      "source_xml": "ppt/slides/slide51.xml",
      "source_xml_sha256": "8f4aeb4f95285e3dfb132b5c6ef74c65da25fe80e49874b6cbc8ab63b4925b8a",
      "paragraphs": [
        "DDPMs",
        "Both forward and reverse processes are formalized as a Markovian processes, therefore:",
        "𝑞(𝒙𝑡∣𝒙𝑡−1, …, 𝒙0)=𝑞(𝒙𝑡∣ 𝒙𝑡−1)",
        "𝑝(𝒙𝑡−1∣𝒙𝑡, …, 𝒙𝑇)=𝑝(𝒙𝑡−1∣𝒙𝑡)",
        "51"
      ],
      "links": []
    },
